│   ├── format-engagement-count.ts   # Formats large numbers (e.g., 1.2K)
│   └── trigger-haptic.ts
├── et-post.tsx          # Main component (Object.assign: Footer, Likes, Comments, Shares, Save, SharedPost, etc.)
├── et-post.test.tsx     # Tests
├── index.ts                # Public exports (includes FooterLikesProps, FooterCommentsProps, etc.)
└── AGENTS.mdc               # This file
```

## Design Decisions

### SharedPost Left Border Strategy

The left border is applied **per-subcomponent** (Header and Body each have `borderLeftWidth: 1`) rather than on the Frame. This allows the Attachment area to render full-width without a left border, matching the Figma design.

### Body Delegates to EtReadMoreText

`PostBody` no longer manages expansion state (hidden text measurement, `isExpanded`, `toggleExpanded`, `shouldShowMore`). All expand/collapse logic is delegated to the `EtReadMoreText` component. The context only carries `maxLines` for text configuration.

### Compound Footer Pattern

Engagement actions use a compound component pattern instead of props on `EtPost`. This was chosen because:

1. **Extensibility** — Adding new actions (e.g., `onCountPress` for opening drawers) doesn't require modifying `EtPostProps` or threading through context
2. **Colocation** — Each action's data (count, handlers, active state) is passed at the point of use, not scattered across 12+ props
3. **Flat naming** — `EtPost.Likes` (not `EtPost.Footer.Likes`) for a simpler API
4. **Auto-layout** — `PostFooter` uses `React.Children.forEach` to detect `FooterSave` by `displayName` and render it on the right; all other children go to the left section
5. **Deduplication** — `FooterAction` is a shared internal component used by Likes/Comments/Shares, handling icon rendering, count formatting (`formatEngagementCount`), haptic feedback, and separate icon/count `Pressable` elements

### Attachment Style Prop Placement

For `LinkPreviewRenderer` and `TradeRenderer`, the `style` prop is applied to the outermost container (not the card), so consumers can control margins/padding without breaking the card's internal layout.

## Do's and Don'ts

### Do's

- Use compound children for custom layouts
- Pass engagement data to footer subcomponents (`EtPost.Likes`, `.Comments`, `.Shares`, `.Save`), not to `EtPost` itself
- Use `onCountPress` on Likes/Comments/Shares when the count tap should do something different from the icon tap (e.g., open a drawer)
- Provide `testID` for testing
- Use `haptics={false}` to disable feedback
- Use `maxLines` to customize expansion threshold
- Place attachment sub-components (`.Image`, `.Video`, `.Link`, `.Trade`, `.Poll`) directly as children of `<EtPost>` (exception: inside a shared post, wrap them in `<EtPost.SharedPost.Attachment>`)
- Use `EtPost.SharedPost` compound sub-components (`.Header`, `.Body`, `.Attachment`) for shared post layouts
- Wrap shared post attachments in `<EtPost.SharedPost.Attachment>` so they get proper border radius clipping

### Don'ts

- Don't pass `likes`, `comments`, `shares`, `isLiked`, `isSaved`, `onLike`, `onComment`, `onShare`, `onSave` as props to `EtPost` — these were removed; use footer subcomponents instead
- Don't use subcomponents outside `<EtPost>` (will throw)
- Don't pass colors directly - use theme via context
- Don't add `!important` to style overrides
- Don't render more than one attachment per post
- Don't manage text expansion state manually - `EtReadMoreText` handles it
- Don't apply the left border on the SharedPost Frame - Header and Body handle it individually
- Don't use `FooterAction` directly — it's an internal component; use `EtPost.Likes`, `.Comments`, `.Shares` instead

## Related Components

- `EtAvatar` - User avatar component (used in SharedPost.Header with `size="small"` + `shape="square"`)
- `EtReadMoreText` - Text expansion component (used by PostBody and SharedPost.Body)
- `EtText` - Foundation text component
- `EtIconV2` - Icon component (used in Footer)

